[Slack](https://slack.com/) is a messaging platform where teams work in channels. Custom Slack apps can add slash commands: when someone runs a command such as `/hello`, Slack sends the request to a URL you choose and shows the response in the conversation.

# How does the integration work?

The **Slack Command Bot** recipe is an Appwrite Function from the [Appwrite templates repository](https://github.com/appwrite/templates/tree/1.2.0/node/slack-command-bot) that serves as the request URL for a slash command. For every request, it checks the `x-slack-signature` header against your app's signing secret and rejects requests older than five minutes, then replies with `Hello World!`. Invalid requests get a `400` response.

Use it as a verified starting point and replace the reply with your own logic.

{% info title="Not in the template picker" %}
This recipe isn't listed under **Functions** > **Templates** in the Appwrite Console. You deploy it yourself from the templates repository, as described below.
{% /info %}

# Prerequisites

- A Slack workspace where you can install apps.
- Git and `tar` on your machine.

# How to implement

To implement the Slack integration, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Create a Slack app

Go to [Slack apps](https://api.slack.com/apps), click **Create New App**, choose **From scratch**, and pick your workspace. On the app's **Basic Information** page, copy the **Signing Secret** under **App Credentials**.

## Step 2: Deploy the recipe

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already.

Download the released recipe and package it as a `.tar.gz` archive:

```bash
git clone --depth 1 --branch 1.2.0 https://github.com/appwrite/templates.git
cd templates/node/slack-command-bot
tar --exclude code.tar.gz -czf code.tar.gz .
```

In the Appwrite Console, go to **Functions**, click **Create function**, and choose **create a function manually**. Pick a Node.js runtime, upload `code.tar.gz`, set the entrypoint to `src/main.js` and the build command to `npm install`, and allow anyone to execute the function so Slack can call its domain. Then click **Create and deploy**.

You can also deploy the folder with the [Appwrite CLI](/docs/tooling/command-line/functions), or copy it into your own repository and [deploy it from Git](/docs/products/functions/deploy-from-git).

## Step 3: Add the signing secret

Open the function's **Settings** tab, go to **Environment variables**, and add `SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET` with the signing secret from step 1. Mark it as a [secret variable](/docs/products/functions/environment-variables#secret-variables), then redeploy the function so the variable takes effect.

## Step 4: Create the slash command

Copy the function's domain from the **Domains** tab. In your Slack app settings, open **Slash Commands** and click **Create New Command**. Enter a command such as `/hello`, paste the function's domain as the **Request URL**, add a short description, and save.

Then open **Install App** and install the app to your workspace.

## Step 5: Test the command

Run `/hello` in any channel of your workspace. Slack shows the `Hello World!` reply, visible only to you because the function responds with plain text. Each request appears in the function's **Executions** tab, and the logs show whether the signature check passed.

